This is the combined Part B paper for Quantum Mechanics and Electromagnetism and Quantum Theory and Quantum Computers. Its cover explicitly prints Part B and the joint label B7.1/C7.1. Section A belongs to the former course; section B belongs to the latter and is also present, with question numbers 1–3, in the separate Part C paper C7.1b, formal code `DMAC 2A78`. The combined paper is organized here under Quantum Mechanics and Electromagnetism; the standalone Part C paper belongs to Quantum Theory and Quantum Computers.

Source release: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/maths_past_papers_3[Oxford examination archive release thread], Part B bundle pages 291–296. The sitting was Wednesday 15 June 2011, 9.30am–12.30pm.

Answer availability checked on 28 October 2026: bounded searches of official Oxford sources by year, course titles, module labels and both papers' formal codes found no advertisement of exact full-paper or selected-part solutions. Availability for these 2011 papers was checked independently of the later even-year policy described in the https://www.maths.ox.ac.uk/members/students/undergraduate-courses/examinations-assessments/overview-examinations[departmental examination overview].

= A
{parent=2011}
{scope}

= 1
{parent=A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Use <International System of Units>, with $c^{-2}=\mu_0\varepsilon_0$. The <Maxwell equations> with <charge density> $\rho$ and <current density> $\mathbf J$ are
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=\rho/\varepsilon_0,\qquad\nabla\cdot\mathbf B=0,\qquad\nabla\times\mathbf E=-\partial_t\mathbf B,\qquad\nabla\times\mathbf B=\mu_0\mathbf J+c^{-2}\partial_t\mathbf E.}
$$
Locally, or in a suitable contractible region, the zero <divergence> of $\mathbf B$ allows a <magnetic vector potential> $\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A$. <Faraday's law> then makes $\mathbf E+\partial_t\mathbf A$ <curl>-free, so a <electric potential> can be chosen with
$$
\boxed{\mathbf E=-\nabla\Phi-\partial_t\mathbf A,\qquad\mathbf B=\nabla\times\mathbf A.}
$$
These formulas automatically satisfy the two homogeneous <Maxwell equations>. The local topology assumption rules out obstructions to expressing a closed field as a <gradient> or <curl>.

For any sufficiently smooth scalar $\chi$, the <gauge transformation>
$$
\boxed{\mathbf A\mapsto\mathbf A+\nabla\chi,\qquad\Phi\mapsto\Phi-\partial_t\chi}
$$
leaves both physical fields unchanged, because <curl> of a <gradient> is zero and spatial and time <derivatives> commute. In such a region this accounts for the freedom between potentials for the same fields.

The <Lorenz gauge> is $\nabla\cdot\mathbf A+c^{-2}\partial_t\Phi=0$. Substitution into the sourced <Maxwell equations>, using $\nabla\times(\nabla\times\mathbf A)=\nabla(\nabla\cdot\mathbf A)-\nabla^2\mathbf A$, gives the decoupled <wave equations>
$$
\boxed{(\nabla^2-c^{-2}\partial_t^2)\Phi=-\rho/\varepsilon_0,\qquad(\nabla^2-c^{-2}\partial_t^2)\mathbf A=-\mu_0\mathbf J.}
$$
For example, Gauss's law gives $-\nabla^2\Phi-\partial_t\nabla\cdot\mathbf A=\rho/\varepsilon_0$; the gauge condition turns this into the scalar equation. The remaining gauge freedom preserving the condition has $(\nabla^2-c^{-2}\partial_t^2)\chi=0$.

Put $\theta=\omega t-Kz$ for the given wave. Direct differentiation gives $\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=0$ and $\nabla\times\mathbf E=K\mathbf E$. Integrating <Faraday's law> in time yields the wave's <magnetic field>
$$
\boxed{\mathbf B=\frac K\omega\,\widehat{\mathbf z}\times\mathbf E=\frac{KE}{\omega}(-\sin\theta,\cos\theta,0).}
$$
One could superpose a time-independent <divergence>-free and <curl>-free <magnetic field>; the expression displayed chooses no such extra background. It has zero <divergence> and $\partial_t\mathbf B=-K\mathbf E$, so the magnetic Gauss and Faraday equations hold. Moreover
$$
\nabla\times\mathbf B=\frac{K^2}{\omega^2}\partial_t\mathbf E.
$$
Since the time <derivative> is nonzero, the vacuum <Ampère-Maxwell law> holds precisely when
$$
\boxed{K^2=\omega^2/c^2.}
$$
This verifies all four source-free equations, including both <divergence> constraints. The sign of $\omega/K$ selects the direction of propagation.

A wave has <circular polarization> when, at a fixed spatial point, its transverse electric <vector> has constant nonzero magnitude and rotates uniformly through a circle. Here $E_x^2+E_y^2=E^2$ and the angle changes at rate $\omega$, so **the wave is circularly polarized**. Its magnetic <vector> is perpendicular to the electric <vector>, has magnitude $|E|/c$ under the <dispersion relation>, and rotates with it. A handedness label additionally requires a convention for the observer's viewing direction.

The <Lorenz-gauge potentials for a circularly polarized plane wave> give a convenient choice. Take $\Phi=0$ and $\mathbf A=\omega^{-2}\partial_t\mathbf E$. Because $\partial_t^2\mathbf E=-\omega^2\mathbf E$,
$$
-\partial_t\mathbf A=\mathbf E,\qquad
\nabla\times\mathbf A=\omega^{-2}\partial_t(K\mathbf E)=\frac K\omega\widehat{\mathbf z}\times\mathbf E=\mathbf B.
$$
Also $\nabla\cdot\mathbf A=\omega^{-2}\partial_t\nabla\cdot\mathbf E=0$. Together with $\partial_t\Phi=0$, this proves **these potentials satisfy the <Lorenz gauge>**. They satisfy the source-free potential <wave equations> because every component has the same <dispersion relation> just obtained.

= 2
{parent=A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Take $m,\omega>0$ and use the usual common invariant domain for the <position operator>, <momentum operator> and their products. Since $X$ and $P$ are <self-adjoint>,
$$
\boxed{a_+=\frac{P+im\omega X}{\sqrt{2m\omega\hbar}}.}
$$
Expanding the <commutator> and using $[P,X]=-i\hbar I$ gives
$$
[a_-,a_+]=\frac{im\omega[P,X]-im\omega[X,P]}{2m\omega\hbar}=I.
$$
The two products are
$$
a_+a_-=\frac{P^2+m^2\omega^2X^2-m\omega\hbar}{2m\omega\hbar},\qquad
a_-a_+=\frac{P^2+m^2\omega^2X^2+m\omega\hbar}{2m\omega\hbar}.
$$
Consequently the <quantum harmonic oscillator> <Hamiltonian operator> is
$$
\boxed{H=\hbar\omega(a_+a_-+\tfrac12 I)=\hbar\omega(a_-a_+-\tfrac12 I).}
$$
The identity $[AB,C]=A[B,C]+[A,C]B$ now gives
$$
\boxed{[H,a_-]=-\hbar\omega a_-,\qquad[H,a_+]=\hbar\omega a_+.}
$$
Thus $H(a_\pm\psi)=(E\pm\hbar\omega)a_\pm\psi$. **Each ladder image is an <eigenstate> if it is nonzero**; a zero <vector> is not an <eigenstate>. The distinction is needed for lowering the <ground state>. Their squared <norms> are
$$
\boxed{\|a_-\psi\|^2=\left(\frac E{\hbar\omega}-\frac12\right)\|\psi\|^2,\qquad
\|a_+\psi\|^2=\left(\frac E{\hbar\omega}+\frac12\right)\|\psi\|^2.}
$$
These follow by moving the operator to the other side of the <inner product> with its <adjoint operator>. The first inequality implies $E\geq\hbar\omega/2$ for every <eigenstate>.

For a lowest-energy <quantum state>, $a_-\psi_0$ must vanish, since otherwise it would be an <eigenstate> with lower energy. The <norm> formula then forces $E_0=\hbar\omega/2$. Raising never vanishes and produces
$$
\boxed{E_n=\hbar\omega(n+\tfrac12),\qquad\psi_n=(a_+)^n\psi_0,\qquad n=0,1,2,\ldots.}
$$
To see that there are no other <eigenvalues>, repeatedly lower any <eigenstate>. If $E/(\hbar\omega)-1/2$ were not a nonnegative integer, there would be a final nonzero lowered <quantum state> with energy strictly between $\hbar\omega/2$ and $3\hbar\omega/2$. Its lower image would have positive <norm> but energy below the lower bound, a contradiction. Thus every <eigenstate> reaches the ground eigenspace after an integer number of lowerings. On a positive level, lowering is injective by its strictly positive <norm> factor. The assumed one-dimensional ground eigenspace therefore makes every eigenspace one-dimensional, so the displayed raised <quantum states> give all <eigenstates> up to nonzero multiples. If $\psi_0$ is normalized, their <norms> are $\|\psi_n\|^2=n!$.

For $k\geq1$, $(a_+)^k\psi$ has <eigenvalue> $E+k\hbar\omega$, distinct from $E$. <eigenstates> of a <self-adjoint operator> with distinct <eigenvalues> are <orthogonal>: applying self-adjointness to their <inner product> gives $(E'-E)\langle\psi,\phi\rangle=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\langle\psi,(a_+)^k\psi\rangle=0.}
$$
The <expectation> of $(a_-)^k$ also vanishes, either by lowering and <orthogonality> or by conjugating the adjoint relation.

Solving the ladder definitions for $X$ and $P$ gives
$$
T=\frac{\hbar\omega}{4}(a_-+a_+)^2,\qquad V=-\frac{\hbar\omega}{4}(a_+-a_-)^2,
$$
so $T-V=\frac12\hbar\omega((a_+)^2+(a_-)^2)$. Both terms have zero <expectation> by the preceding <orthogonality>. Since $T+V=H$, the oscillator form of the <quantum virial theorem> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E_\psi(T)=\mathbb E_\psi(V)=E/2.}
$$
Expectations here are divided by $\|\psi\|^2$ if the <eigenstate> is not normalized; no <normalization> assumption was used in the ladder argument.

= 3
{parent=A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

For a nonzero <quantum state> $\psi$ and a <self-adjoint operator> $A$, the <expectation value> and <standard deviation> of its measurement are
$$
\boxed{a=\mathbb E_\psi(A)=\frac{\langle\psi,A\psi\rangle}{\langle\psi,\psi\rangle},\qquad
\Delta_\psi(A)=\frac{\|(A-aI)\psi\|}{\|\psi\|}=\sqrt{\mathbb E_\psi(A^2)-a^2}.}
$$
The squared deviation is also called the <variance> of the <observable>. All displayed products are understood on <quantum states> for which the relevant moments exist. Self-adjointness gives $\overline{\langle\psi,A\psi\rangle}=\langle A\psi,\psi\rangle=\langle\psi,A\psi\rangle$, so $a$ is real. Expanding the squared <norm> gives the <variance> expression. A <norm> is nonnegative and is zero exactly for the zero <vector>, hence $\Delta_\psi(A)=0$ precisely when $A\psi=a\psi$. Thus **zero dispersion is equivalent to being an <eigenstate>**.

For bounded $H$, the <operator exponential> is the <norm>-convergent power series
$$
U_t=\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{(-itH/\hbar)^n}{n!}.
$$
For a general unbounded <self-adjoint> <Hamiltonian operator>, the <spectral theorem> defines the same notation by
$$
U_t=\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-itE/\hbar}\,dP_H(E).
$$
This definition does not require the power series to converge on every <quantum state>. <complex conjugation> of the spectral multiplier gives $U_t^*=U_{-t}$, and multiplication of the phases gives $U_{-t}U_t=I$. Thus **$U_t$ is <unitary>**, preserves the <quantum state> <norm>, and has $U_0=I$.

For initial data in the domain of $H$, differentiating the spectral expression yields $\partial_tU_t\psi_0=-(i/\hbar)HU_t\psi_0$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\psi_t=U_t\psi_0}
$$
solves the <Schrödinger equation> with the prescribed initial <quantum state>. Uniqueness follows by differentiating $U_{-t}\psi_t$: the two terms cancel, leaving the constant $\psi_0$.

In the two-dimensional energy <basis>, expand the initial <quantum state> as $\psi_0=c_1\Psi_1+c_2\Psi_2$. Each energy <eigenvector> receives its own phase under the <operator exponential>, giving
$$
\boxed{\psi_t=c_1e^{-itE_1/\hbar}\Psi_1+c_2e^{-itE_2/\hbar}\Psi_2.}
$$
This is the general solution because every initial <vector> has a unique <basis> expansion.

In that <orthonormal basis> the other <observable> has the real symmetric <matrix>
$$
[A]=\begin{pmatrix}\cos2\alpha&\sin2\alpha\\\sin2\alpha&-\cos2\alpha\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Its conjugate transpose equals itself, so it is <self-adjoint>. Also $A^2=I$ and $\operatorname{tr}A=0$, giving <eigenvalues> $+1$ and $-1$. Direct substitution gives the normalized <orthogonal> <eigenvectors>
$$
\boxed{v_+=\cos\alpha\,\Psi_1+\sin\alpha\,\Psi_2,\qquad
v_-=-\sin\alpha\,\Psi_1+\cos\alpha\,\Psi_2.}
$$
An irrelevant global phase may multiply either <vector>. With the chosen lower <eigenstate> initially,
$$
\boxed{\psi_t=-\sin\alpha\,e^{-itE_1/\hbar}\Psi_1+\cos\alpha\,e^{-itE_2/\hbar}\Psi_2.}
$$
The <Born rule> says that the <probability> for the higher <eigenstate> is $|\langle v_+,\psi_t\rangle|^2$. Its amplitude is $\sin\alpha\cos\alpha(e^{-itE_2/\hbar}-e^{-itE_1/\hbar})$. Using $|e^{-iu}-e^{-iv}|^2=4\sin^2((u-v)/2)$ gives
$$
\boxed{P_+(t)=\sin^2(2\alpha)\sin^2\left(\frac{(E_2-E_1)t}{2\hbar}\right).}
$$
This is the <transition probability for a rotated two-level observable>. Its time dependence comes from the relative phase between two energy <eigenstates>. If their energies coincide, or if the measured <observable> has the energy <eigenvectors> as its <eigenvectors>, this transition <probability> is zero at all times.

= B
{parent=2011}
{scope}

= 4
{parent=B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

In the scalar two-particle model, the <fermionic exchange symmetry> and <bosonic exchange symmetry> conditions are
$$
\boxed{\Psi(x_2,x_1)=-\Psi(x_1,x_2)\text{ for fermions},\qquad
\Psi(x_2,x_1)=\Psi(x_1,x_2)\text{ for bosons}.}
$$
For particles with spin, exchange acts on all their labels; the stated coordinate-only model fixes the spatial symmetry sector. Define $S\Psi(x_1,x_2)=\Psi(x_2,x_1)$. Equal masses make the kinetic operator invariant under exchange, and the assumed potential is invariant too, so $HS=SH$. Therefore $H\Psi=E\Psi$ implies $H(S\Psi)=E(S\Psi)$. The components
$$
\Psi_{\mathrm B}=\tfrac12(\Psi+S\Psi),\qquad
\Psi_{\mathrm F}=\tfrac12(\Psi-S\Psi)
$$
are respectively symmetric and antisymmetric eigenfunctions whenever nonzero. Their sum is the original nonzero <quantum state>, so at least one is nonzero. An eigenspace may contain both sectors if the energy is degenerate.

The <normal modes of two harmonically coupled identical particles> provide the separation. The <coordinate transformation> to $y_+,y_-$ is <orthogonal>, with absolute Jacobian one. Exchanging the particles leaves $y_+$ fixed and changes $y_-$ to $-y_-$. Hence **bosonic <quantum states> are even and fermionic <quantum states> odd in the relative coordinate**. The <chain rule> gives
$$
\partial_{x_1}=\frac{\partial_{y_+}+\partial_{y_-}}{\sqrt2},\qquad
\partial_{x_2}=\frac{\partial_{y_+}-\partial_{y_-}}{\sqrt2},\qquad
\partial_{x_1}^2+\partial_{x_2}^2=\partial_{y_+}^2+\partial_{y_-}^2.
$$
Also $x_1^2+x_2^2=y_+^2+y_-^2$ and $(x_1-x_2)^2=2y_-^2$. Combining the two trapping potentials with the interaction therefore gives the separated <Hamiltonian operator>
$$
H=\left[-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\partial_{y_+}^2+\frac12m\omega^2y_+^2\right]
+\left[-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\partial_{y_-}^2+\frac12m\omega^2\cos^2\alpha\,y_-^2\right].
$$
These are independent oscillators of frequencies $\omega$ and $\omega\cos\alpha$. Both frequencies are positive in the stated range. Their product <eigenstates> have energies
$$
\boxed{E_{n_+,n_-}=\hbar\omega\left[n_++\tfrac12+(n_-+\tfrac12)\cos\alpha\right],\qquad n_+,n_-=0,1,2,\ldots.}
$$
The <parity> of an oscillator level is $(-1)^n$. Consequently <bosons> allow even $n_-$ and <fermions> odd $n_-$, while $n_+$ is unrestricted.

The bosonic <ground state> uses $n_+=n_-=0$, and the fermionic <ground state> uses $n_+=0,n_-=1$. The oscillator length in the relative mode is
$$
\boxed{\tau^2=\frac{\hbar}{m\omega\cos\alpha}=\frac{\hbar\sec\alpha}{m\omega}.}
$$
Using the normalized <Gaussian> for each mode gives
$$
\boxed{\Phi_{\mathrm B}(y_+,y_-)=\psi_\sigma(y_+)\psi_\tau(y_-),\qquad
\Phi_{\mathrm F}(y_+,y_-)=\psi_\sigma(y_+)\phi_\tau(y_-).}
$$
Indeed $\phi_\tau(y)=\sqrt2\,y\psi_\tau(y)/\tau$ by differentiating the <Gaussian>, so it is the normalized odd first <excited state>. The ground energies are $E_{\mathrm B}=\hbar\omega(1+\cos\alpha)/2$ and $E_{\mathrm F}=\hbar\omega(1+3\cos\alpha)/2$.

Choose normalized factors for a normalized product <quantum state>; this can always be done by reciprocal rescaling of its factors. Since the coordinate Jacobian has absolute value one,
$$
\mathbb E[f(y_-)]=\iint f(y_-)\,|\psi(y_+)|^2|\phi(y_-)|^2\,dy_+dy_-
=\boxed{\int f(y)|\phi(y)|^2\,dy.}
$$
If the supplied factors are not individually normalized, the general expression has an additional factor $\|\psi\|^2$; choosing normalized factors is the convention in the displayed formula.

For the <ground states>, put $\rho(y)=|\psi_\tau(y)|^2=e^{-y^2/\tau^2}/(\sqrt\pi\tau)$. Then $|\phi_\tau(y)|^2=2y^2\rho(y)/\tau^2$ and $\rho'=-2y\rho/\tau^2$. Thus, for differentiable $f$ with suitable <Gaussian> integrability and vanishing boundary terms,
$$
\mathbb E_{\mathrm F}[f]=\frac2{\tau^2}\int y^2f(y)\rho(y)\,dy
=-\int yf(y)\rho'(y)\,dy
=\boxed{\int\bigl(f(y)+yf'(y)\bigr)\rho(y)\,dy.}
$$
This is <integration by parts>, and every polynomial satisfies the required endpoint conditions. The <relative-coordinate moments in even and odd oscillator ground states> follow by substituting $f(y)=y^n$:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E_{\mathrm F}[y_-^n]=(n+1)\mathbb E_{\mathrm B}[y_-^n].}
$$
For odd $n$, both expectations vanish by <parity>, so this is an equality of zeros rather than an increase in signed mean separation. The even moments show the physical difference. In particular $\mathbb E_{\mathrm B}[y_-^2]=\tau^2/2$ and $\mathbb E_{\mathrm F}[y_-^2]=3\tau^2/2$, so the fermionic root-mean-square particle separation is $\sqrt3$ times the bosonic value. The fermionic <probability density> vanishes at coincidence $y_-=0$, expressing <Pauli exclusion principle> in this spatial symmetry sector. This is an exchange constraint on the <wave function>, not an additional classical force. As $\alpha$ approaches $\pi/2$, the relative oscillator softens and its width diverges; the excluded endpoint would no longer have this normalizable oscillator <ground state>.

= 5
{parent=B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Use an expansion for the <eigenstate> continuing the nondegenerate level, with intermediate <normalization> $\langle\psi_0,\psi(\epsilon)\rangle=1$:
$$
E(\epsilon)=E_0+\epsilon E^{(1)}+\epsilon^2E^{(2)}+O(\epsilon^3),\qquad
\psi(\epsilon)=\psi_0+\epsilon\chi+\epsilon^2\eta+\cdots,\qquad\langle\psi_0,\chi\rangle=0.
$$
Insert these expressions into the <eigenvalue> equation. At first order,
$$
(H_0-E_0)\chi=-(H'-E^{(1)})\psi_0.
$$
Taking the <inner product> with $\psi_0$ eliminates the left side by self-adjointness. Thus
$$
\boxed{E^{(1)}=\langle\psi_0,H'\psi_0\rangle,\qquad
E(\epsilon)=\langle\psi_0,H\psi_0\rangle+O(\epsilon^2).}
$$
For $j\ne0$, projection onto $\psi_j$ instead gives
$$
\langle\psi_j,\chi\rangle=\frac{\langle\psi_j,H'\psi_0\rangle}{E_0-E_j}.
$$
At second order, projecting the <eigenvalue> equation onto $\psi_0$ gives $E^{(2)}=\langle\psi_0,H'\chi\rangle$. Expand $\chi$ in the orthonormal energy <basis> and use self-adjointness of $H'$:
$$
\boxed{\epsilon^2E^{(2)}=\epsilon^2\sum_{j\ne0}\frac{|\langle\psi_j,H'\psi_0\rangle|^2}{E_0-E_j}.}
$$
Nondegeneracy ensures the denominators for this level do not vanish; convergence and validity of the expansion are among the assumed perturbation-theory hypotheses.

For the <cosine perturbation of the ground level in a one-dimensional box>, write $u=\pi x/a$. The product-to-sum identity gives
$$
\cos(2u)\sin u=\tfrac12\bigl(\sin(3u)-\sin u\bigr),
$$
so, with the given common <normalization> of the sine eigenfunctions,
$$
\boxed{V\phi_1=\frac\epsilon2(\phi_3-\phi_1).}
$$
The diagonal <matrix> element is $-\epsilon/2$, and the only nonzero off-diagonal <matrix> element out of the <ground state> is $\langle\phi_3,V\phi_1\rangle=\epsilon/2$. Let $E_{\mathrm{box}}=\pi^2\hbar^2/(2ma^2)$. Since $\mathcal E_3=9E_{\mathrm{box}}$, the ground energy is
$$
E_{\mathrm{ground}}=E_{\mathrm{box}}-\frac\epsilon2+\frac{\epsilon^2/4}{E_{\mathrm{box}}-9E_{\mathrm{box}}}+O(\epsilon^3)
=\boxed{\frac{\pi^2\hbar^2}{2ma^2}-\frac\epsilon2-\frac{ma^2\epsilon^2}{16\pi^2\hbar^2}+O(\epsilon^3).}
$$
The second-order term is negative, as expected for a nondegenerate ground level: every excited-<quantum state> denominator is negative. The first-order shift is also negative for positive $\epsilon$, because the ground-<quantum state> density is largest near the center, where this perturbing potential is negative.

= 6
{parent=B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Use the <Dirac notation> convention that the <inner product> is linear in the ket. Orthonormality of the computational <basis> gives
$$
\langle k|f\rangle=\sum_{\ell\in\mathbb Z_N}f(\ell)\langle k|\ell\rangle=f(k).
$$
The finite geometric sum satisfies $\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\lambda^{rk}=N$ if $r=0$ in $\mathbb Z_N$, and zero otherwise: in the second case it equals $(1-\lambda^{rN})/(1-\lambda^r)=0$. Therefore
$$
\langle\widehat m|\widehat n\rangle=\frac1N\sum_k\lambda^{(n-m)k}=\delta_{mn}.
$$
There are $N$ such orthonormal <vectors> in an $N$-dimensional space, so they form a <basis>. The inverse expansion is verified by the same sum:
$$
\boxed{\frac1{\sqrt N}\sum_j\lambda^{-jk}|\widehat j\rangle
=\frac1N\sum_{j,\ell}\lambda^{j(\ell-k)}|\ell\rangle=|k\rangle.}
$$
This is the <unitary> <discrete Fourier transform> and its inverse.

For the <basis-copying isometry>, the images $|k\rangle|k\rangle$ are orthonormal, so $C$ is a linear <isometry>, with $C^*C=I$. Its <matrix> elements give
$$
\langle Ck|\,|f\rangle|g\rangle
=\langle k|f\rangle\langle k|g\rangle=f(k)g(k)=\langle k|f.g\rangle.
$$
Applying the definition of the <adjoint operator>, the coefficient of $|k\rangle$ in $C^*|f\rangle|g\rangle$ is exactly this expression. Thus
$$
\boxed{C^*|f\rangle|g\rangle=|f.g\rangle.}
$$
Equivalently $C^*|k\rangle|\ell\rangle=\delta_{k\ell}|k\rangle$. The adjoint of <basis> copying acts as pointwise multiplication of coefficient <functions>.

For the <Fourier transform of a basis-copying isometry>, the <normalization> matters:
$$
f_m(k)f_n(k)=\frac{\lambda^{(m+n)k}}N=\frac1{\sqrt N}f_{m+n}(k).
$$
Hence $C^*|\widehat m\rangle|\widehat n\rangle=N^{-1/2}|\widehat{m+n}\rangle$. Taking adjoint <matrix> elements, or expanding both output factors in the inverse Fourier <basis>, yields
$$
\boxed{C|\widehat j\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt N}\sum_{m+n=j}|\widehat m\rangle|\widehat n\rangle.}
$$
**The printed formula is missing the factor $1/\sqrt N$.** There are $N$ mutually <orthogonal> terms in its sum, so without that factor its right side has <norm> $\sqrt N$, whereas its left side has <norm> one because $C$ is an <isometry>. The formula above is the normalized identity consistent with all the preceding definitions. For $N=1$ the distinction disappears, but the no-cloning assertion is substantive for $N\geq2$.

The normalized output is not $|\widehat j\rangle|\widehat j\rangle$. Its coefficient <matrix> pairs every Fourier label $m$ with $j-m$; tracing out either factor gives $I/N$. Thus it is a <maximally entangled state>, while two clones of a pure Fourier <quantum state> would have pure reduced <quantum states>. A copier for the computational <basis> therefore does not copy the Fourier <basis>.

More generally, suppose a linear <isometry> with a fixed blank input could clone every normalized <quantum state>. Its restriction to $|\psi\rangle|\mathrm{blank}\rangle$ would define a linear map $D$ with $D|k\rangle=|k\rangle|k\rangle$ on every computational <basis> <quantum state>. Linearity forces $D=C$, which has just been shown to fail on Fourier <quantum states>. This proves the <no-cloning theorem> in the pure-<quantum state> isometric setting.

The inner-product proof makes the obstruction independent of the chosen <basis>. If an <isometry> cloned two <quantum states> with overlap $s=\langle\psi|\phi\rangle$, preservation of inner products would give $s=s^2$, or, allowing irrelevant output phases, $|s|=|s|^2$. Distinct nonorthogonal rays have $0<|s|<1$, which is impossible. Even allowing an output environment does not help: <unitary> evolution would give $|s|=|s|^2|\langle e_\psi|e_\phi\rangle|\leq|s|^2$, the same contradiction. **Arbitrary unknown <quantum states> cannot be cloned**, although any fixed <orthonormal basis> can be copied and a one-dimensional <quantum state> space is trivial.
